contentText:
A rescued mink from a Utah fur farm Julie Leopo for ProPublica

The strain of bird flu that&#8217;s ripped through American barns for the last four years has killed two people who caught it from sick animals. But as far as anyone can tell, humans can&#8217;t spread it. The virus has remained a true &#8220;bird&#8221; flu, tailored to attach to avian cells, not invade human throats and noses, where coughing and sneezing reliably spew droplets.&nbsp;

Under the right conditions, however, that could change. A single mutation could give the bird flu a foothold in people and spark a pandemic that experts fear could be deadlier than COVID-19.&nbsp;

For that to happen, this bird flu would likely need to find an intermediate host — an animal vulnerable to viruses that infect both birds and humans. These animals would be crowded together, each infection raising the odds that a new strain could arise through mutation. Then, for that strain to jump across species, it would have to have access to humans and time to spread among them undetected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

That perfect petri dish exists right now, a ProPublica investigation found. For over two months, an outbreak of bird flu has raged uncontrolled on a Utah farm that raises thousands of mink for fur. Mink have lungs and airways that on a cellular level are remarkably similar to those of people; they have caught and spread other flus and infected some humans with COVID-19.&nbsp;

Instead of trying to stop the outbreak and prevent it from spilling into humans, state and federal officials have done little except try to keep its existence quiet, our investigation shows. The failure to act amounts to a cascade of missteps flu experts called “irresponsible’’ and “puzzling.’’ One called it “playing with fire.’’

C&T Fenn Farms, a mink fur farm west of Provo, Utah, on Sept. 25 Julie Leopo for ProPublica

Utah agriculture officials learned in mid-August that mink were dying of bird flu on C&T Fenn Farms, about an hour’s drive south from Salt Lake City, according to communications released under the state’s public records law.&nbsp;

Had poultry been infected, the playbook would have been clear: The strain is so virulent that if a single chicken tests positive, the U.S. government requires farmers to kill the whole flock to protect other farms as well as public health. Poultry farmers are reimbursed for the loss.

But there are no federal rules for how to deal with sick mink.&nbsp;

Handling Utah’s response was state veterinarian Amanda Price, who sought help from the U.S. Department of Agriculture, which takes the lead on bird flu outbreaks involving poultry and cows.&nbsp;

USDA officials said they didn’t regulate mink or have a vaccine that was approved to give them. And though officials with the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention spoke with Price’s health department counterparts who were less involved with the outbreak, they never talked to her, and she never reached out.

The threat of spillover has been far from the minds of Utah officials, records and interviews show. Instead, front and center have been the interests of a business raising animals to skin for luxury pelts to ship overseas.&nbsp;

&#8220;It has been devastating,&#8221; Price told ProPublica of the outbreak and its impact on the farm’s owner. &#8220;This is his life&#8217;s work that he&#8217;s seeing be destroyed. I really hope that you understand the humanity behind this situation.&#8221;

Flu experts were unequivocal that the government should have ordered the farm to cull its mink, as other countries have done .&nbsp;

But Utah never seriously considered it. A fur trade representative warned Price that doing so would kill the business and, ultimately, the industry. Farms spend decades breeding mink to develop unique genetic lineages.&nbsp;

&#8220;There&#8217;s just not a lot of options out there other than kind of sitting and waiting and seeing what happens,” Price said, noting that USDA officials did not urge her to kill the mink or make her feel like bird flu in mink was any riskier than infections in other animals that have caught it, like ducks and cows.

Flu experts found that response surprising.

“Every other country that I&#8217;m aware of that has had H5N1 in fur farms, depopulation has happened pretty quickly,” said Tom Peacock, an avian influenza researcher at the Pirbright Institute. &#8220;The fact that the first instinct wasn&#8217;t just to depopulate and figure stuff out later is very concerning to me.”

And all the while, officials didn&#8217;t monitor the mink workers for symptoms, as guidance from the CDC urges.&nbsp;

Their exposure is significant: Mink sneeze. They escape. They attack. “The mink are fierce,” said a Spanish-speaking “minkero” who works on a nearby farm, showing the scars he got from the aggressive, solitary creatures. “You get scared because the fucking thing bites, and it claws at you.”

A mink farmworker, sometimes referred to as a “minkero” among the Spanish-speaking community, shows scars from mink bites and scratches in Payson, Utah. Workers typically wear long gloves to protect themselves but can still be bitten or scratched. Julie Leopo for ProPublica

Right now in Washington state, officials are working to track down the contacts of a symptomatic person exposed to dairy cattle infected with bird flu. The patient is isolating at home. Utah public health officials used to do similar, proactive monitoring. But they stopped after complaints from their colleagues in the agriculture department that farmers were annoyed by it.

“Us doing active monitoring was making it so that farms didn’t want to work with them,” said Pamela Gomez Pinedo, a respiratory disease epidemiologist for Utah&#8217;s health department. As a result, public health workers had a &#8220;pretty difficult time&#8221; getting details from agriculture officials, she said.

As part of a 2025 agreement , the Utah Department of Agriculture and Food agreed to share information about bird flu outbreaks on farms — so long as the health officials didn’t do anything to track workers’ health.&nbsp;

&#8220;We will only do active monitoring in the case of evidence of human-to-human transmission,” Pinedo said in emails with a CDC epidemiologist. As the outbreak stretched to a month, CDC officials expressed concerns, asking how anyone would know if a worker had been infected if they weren’t getting tested.

Price said the state’s policy not to monitor workers is meant to allow farmers to decide whether to contact the health department, rather than “forcing it on them.” &#8220;If we started seeing people actively dying from this, obviously that would change how we respond to this,” she said.&nbsp;

A telltale sign of bird flu infection in humans is eye redness. It is present in over 90% of cases and is often the sole symptom a person displays.&nbsp;

Last Saturday, a ProPublica reporter tracked down two people who worked at the Utah mink farm. One had a visibly red eye.&nbsp;

The CDC explicitly urges testing for the symptom .&nbsp;

After ProPublica reached out to the farm, its owner, Chad Fenn, contacted the Utah County Health Department about the red eye, which had cleared up by Monday, he told ProPublica. He said the worker got dust in his eye; the department didn&#8217;t recommend testing.

&#8220;They&#8217;re not going to do anything,” Fenn said. “They said they don&#8217;t see that there&#8217;s a need.&#8221; He said the department recommended testing only if other symptoms, like a cough or sore throat, developed.&nbsp;

A half dozen flu experts had no shortage of adjectives to describe their disbelief at the government decisions not to cull the mink or monitor and test the workers.&nbsp;

“It’s amazing to me,” said Gail Hansen, a former Kansas state public health veterinarian and epidemiologist. “Utah doesn&#8217;t want to be the epicenter of the next human flu outbreak, right?”

